<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <div class="pt-faq" style="max-width:800px;margin:0 auto">
    <details><summary>Can Ayurvedic treatment help with cervical spondylosis?</summary><p class="faq-a">Ayurvedic cervical spondylosis treatment in Hyderabad at KSAC Hospitals addresses the factors behind the degeneration rather than pain alone. Care begins with a detailed diagnosis, including a review of any existing X-rays or MRI scans, after which the treating doctor prepares a customised plan that may combine physician-supervised therapy, internal Ayurvedic medicines and guided physiotherapy. Results vary by individual at this <a href="/" style="color:#3c8dbc">Ayurvedic hospital in Hyderabad</a> and progress is tracked through periodic assessment of neck mobility, pain levels and neurological status.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Will I be given the same treatment as other neck pain patients?</summary><p class="faq-a">No. Two patients with the same complaint may need very different plans, because the segments involved, the degree of degeneration, nerve involvement, occupation and associated conditions all differ. The plan is decided after diagnosis, is specific to that patient and is explained by the treating doctor before treatment begins.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is cervical spondylosis the same as a slipped disc?</summary><p class="faq-a">They are related but not the same. Cervical spondylosis refers to the overall degenerative changes in the neck, including disc dehydration, bone spur formation and joint wear. A cervical disc herniation, commonly called a slipped disc, is a specific event where disc material pushes beyond its normal boundary and may press on a nerve root or the spinal cord. A herniation can occur as part of the spondylotic process. The treating doctor distinguishes between the two during diagnosis so the plan fits the actual finding.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is neck pain ayurvedic treatment suitable for people with radiating arm pain?</summary><p class="faq-a">Radiating arm pain is one of the common presentations seen at KSAC Hospitals. The treating doctor first establishes which nerve root is involved, using clinical examination and imaging where needed and then plans care accordingly. Because nerve involvement changes what is appropriate, these cases are assessed carefully before any therapy is advised.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I need to bring my X-ray or MRI to the consultation?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes, where available. X-rays, MRI scans, blood work and current prescriptions help the doctor arrive at a precise diagnosis and plan treatment accordingly. Cervical spondylosis ayurveda care at KSAC Hospitals is planned around clinical findings and the patient's own reports and fresh imaging is advised only where it is needed.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What lifestyle changes support cervical spondylosis recovery?</summary><p class="faq-a">Postural correction, an ergonomic workstation, regular neck strengthening and dietary adjustment are all important for long-term management. Patients are advised on screen height and distance, pillow selection, sleeping posture and breaking up long periods of sitting. Dietary guidance favours warm, nourishing, well-cooked food over cold, dry and processed items. The treating doctor gives individualised recommendations based on the patient's condition and constitution.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>

